.SH NAME
.B hsh - simple shell
- Simple shell program with custom functions created in C, to interface with the operating system's file system.

.SH SYNOPSIS
	./hsh
	$[executable file] [arguments]

.SH DESCRIPTION
	The simple shell program runs a instance of a shell. It displays the character ($) as a prompt and waits for user input.
	The program will run executable files given a valid name (or path) and its arguments, and will run a certain amount of inbuilt custom shell functions.

.SH OPTIONS
	All executables found in $PATH
	The following are the implemented custom commands:
		- env: prints the current working environment.
		- setenv [name] [value]: sets or overwrites an environment variable given its name and value.

.SH SEE ALSO
	$PATH

.SH EXAMPLES
	<current_terminal_prompt> ./hsh
	$ls -l
	$/bin/ls
	$env
	$setenv HOME name

.SH BUGS
	Memory leak present when setting or overwriting environment variables, this bug is consistent with glibc set env behavior.
	Pressing "Ctrl D" twice consecutively, when text has been inputted by user, will cause hsh to exit. This is not consistent with common implementations of a shell.

